WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.127
Good morning, Hank, It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:03.000
So this is a video about mental illness

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.126
I think I'm gonna do it in the format, actually

00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:08.921
So I've had a rough couple months 
My OCD and anxiety have been really high

00:00:08.921 --> 00:00:12.000
and I've also had this lengthy bout of depression

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.101
It started about nine weeks ago 
amid really good news, actually

00:00:16.101 --> 00:00:17.692
Our community had this huge win

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:21.967
We helped encourage this company, Danaher, 
to lower the price of its tuberculosis tests

00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:27.203
which is going to allow 5 million more 
people to be tested for TB every year

00:00:27.203 --> 00:00:28.683
It's incredible

00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:31.671
But when that news happened, I just,
I felt nothing but dread

00:00:31.671 --> 00:00:34.830
It was just this, like, terrible, indescribable fear

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.502
In fact, the indescribability is 
part of the problem, right?

00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:41.906
Because that's part of what makes 
it so difficult to share

00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:44.857
or to feel unalone in

00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:47.862
I can really only approach it 
through metaphor or simile

00:00:47.926 --> 00:00:49.512
It felt like a...

00:00:49.512 --> 00:00:52.825
blanket of dread just descended

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:57.000
Honestly, I don't know what else there is 
to say about it. It just kind of sucks.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.704
When I was in my 20s, there was some 
naive part of me that thought

00:00:59.704 --> 00:01:03.808
if I could just make it to middle age, 
all of this would be behind me

00:01:03.808 --> 00:01:05.858
And that hasn't turned out to be the case

00:01:05.858 --> 00:01:09.878
But what is the case is that 
even when I can't feel it,

00:01:09.878 --> 00:01:13.508
I understand intellectually that this is temporary

00:01:13.508 --> 00:01:18.574
and that this isn't telling the full story of 
what it means to be a person in the world

00:01:18.574 --> 00:01:24.861
And I know that if I just keep pursuing treatment, work with my doctor, take my medicine, move my body when I can, all that stuff,

00:01:24.861 --> 00:01:27.064
eventually I will feel different

00:01:27.064 --> 00:01:28.854
I do think I might be starting to get better.

00:01:28.892 --> 00:01:32.819
It's been a little better in the last week.
That's why I'm able to make a video at all.

00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:35.833
But there have been a lot of false dawns, 
and you never know.

00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:37.858
Speaking of which, let's get a check on the dawn.

00:01:37.954 --> 00:01:43.445
So it's like too gray and rainy for there to 
even  be a visible dawn

00:01:43.445 --> 00:01:47.453
which you know, as a metaphor is a 
little bit on the nose.

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:51.188
Susan Sontag described depression as 
melancholy minus its charms

00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:55.446
which I like a lot because 
first it acknowledges the charms of melancholy

00:01:55.446 --> 00:01:58.616
Like when you're driving alone at night,

00:01:58.616 --> 00:02:03.324
there's something sad about it, but 
there's also something beautiful and lovely about it

00:02:03.324 --> 00:02:07.701
Like when I feel melancholic, there's something 
beautiful and rich and productive about it.

00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:15.484
Whereas when I feel depressed, everything is stolen away 
except for the sadness and the weight on my chest

00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:19.626
I worry this video is going to suck 
because, like, the thing that it's about sucks

00:02:19.627 --> 00:02:25.025
It's just like a piano that's off key, 
playing one note over and over

00:02:25.025 --> 00:02:27.835
despair, despair, despair

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:31.691
But I have to reject the idea that 
that tells the whole story,

00:02:31.691 --> 00:02:33.396
because it just doesn't

00:02:33.396 --> 00:02:37.389
A young woman I knew a little, 
Casey McIntyre, just died of cancer.

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:41.131
She was married to my friend 
Andrew Rose Gregory of the Gregory brothers,

00:02:41.163 --> 00:02:46.537
and she also worked for many years at the same 
publishing house where I published my books.

00:02:46.537 --> 00:02:50.076
Anyway, in lieu of, like, flowers 
at a memorial service or whatever

00:02:50.076 --> 00:02:53.523
what she really wanted to do was 
erase people's medical debts

00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:57.632
One of the many insanities of the 
US Healthcare system is that

00:02:57.632 --> 00:03:01.433
so many people are bankrupted 
just for being sick

00:03:01.433 --> 00:03:05.162
And so Casey wanted to have this, like,  debt jubilee

00:03:05.162 --> 00:03:11.728
And incredibly, people have raised enough money 
to cancel $50 million of medical debt

00:03:11.728 --> 00:03:14.236
over the last few days in Casey's memory

00:03:14.236 --> 00:03:17.244
I'll put a link in the doobly do if you want to join us in donating

00:03:17.244 --> 00:03:21.690
But what a lovely way to remember someone 
and to take care of each other.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.317
To be honest with you, sometimes 
I just hate the world

00:03:24.317 --> 00:03:27.106
The hopelessness feels so complete

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:34.084
But then think of all these people coming together to cancel 
$50 million in medical debt in memory of a lovely person.

00:03:34.084 --> 00:03:38.392
To me, that's the complex story of being a person

00:03:38.392 --> 00:03:42.688
We cannot save those we love from suffering 
as much as we desperately want to

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:45.000
We cannot make life fair or just

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.751
But we can reach out to each other, 
help each other, cancel each other's debts

00:03:49.751 --> 00:03:52.267
We can be careful of each other

00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:57.414
As Philip Larkin put it: 
we can be kind while there is still time

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.410
Hank, I'll see you on Friday. 

